# Tutorial 13 — Camera and Staging
# How to frame scenes, position characters, and use camera movement for storytelling.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_13_camera_staging
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Where you put the camera changes everything about how a scene feels.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Same dialogue, same characters — but zoom in and it's intimate. Pull back and it's lonely. Let's explore.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> Character positions -> section_positions
> Staging for two characters -> section_two_char
> Camera movement -> section_camera_move
> Zoom and focus -> section_zoom
> Composition tips -> section_composition
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Character Positions ─────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_positions
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The engine has several built-in positions for characters on screen:
narrator: {b}far_left{/b} — edge of the frame, partially cut off. Good for eavesdropping or background characters.
narrator: {b}left{/b} — a comfortable conversation position.
narrator: {b}center{/b} — the focus of the scene. Use for solo moments or direct address.
narrator: {b}right{/b} — the other half of a conversation.
narrator: {b}far_right{/b} — opposite edge, mirror of far_left.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: You can also use custom positions with normalized coordinates:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender 0.3,0.85 talking]{/color}
Lavender: That places me at 30% from the left, 85% from the top. Useful for non-standard framing.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Start with the named positions. Graduate to custom coordinates when you need something specific.
> Next: Two characters -> section_two_char
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Staging Two Characters ──────────────────────────────────────

@label section_two_char
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: The classic setup: two characters talking. Left and right.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show lavender left talking]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[show alex right talking]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: When one character speaks, keep the other visible but in a neutral expression. It sells the illusion of presence.
Lavender: Swap who's on which side to signal power shifts or surprise:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move lavender right easeInOut 400]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[move alex left easeInOut 400]{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The side swap tells the player something changed — even if you never say it directly.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Avoid putting both characters on the same side. It reads as confusion, not intention.
> Next: Camera movement -> section_camera_move
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Camera Movement ─────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_camera_move
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Camera movement adds life to static scenes. A slow pan during dialogue creates the feeling of a film.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera pan 0,0 100,0 easeInOut 1000]{/color}
Lavender: That pans the camera from left to right over one second. The characters stay put — the world moves behind them.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Camera movement works best when it's motivated:
narrator: {b}Pan{/b} when the player's attention should shift — a new character enters, or a detail is revealed.
narrator: {b}Stay still{/b} during emotional dialogue — movement would be distracting.
narrator: {b}Combine with character movement{/b} for tracking shots — the camera follows the character.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: A common mistake is moving the camera during every line. Stillness is powerful. Save movement for moments that earn it.
> Next: Zoom -> section_zoom
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Zoom and Focus ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_zoom
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}Zoom{/b} changes the emotional distance between the player and the scene.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera zoom 1.0 1.2 easeIn 600]{/color}
Lavender: That's a subtle 20% zoom in. Small, but the player feels it — the scene becomes more personal.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Zoom levels and when to use them:
narrator: {b}1.0{/b} — neutral. The default. Good for most dialogue.
narrator: {b}1.1–1.2{/b} — slight close-up. Use for emotional beats, confessions, tension.
narrator: {b}1.3+{/b} — dramatic. Use sparingly — for revelations, breakdowns, key decisions.
narrator: {b}0.8–0.9{/b} — pulled back. Use for establishing shots or showing the full scene.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Always reset the zoom when the moment passes. A lingering zoom feels like a bug.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[camera zoom 1.2 1.0 easeOut 400]{/color}
> Next: Composition tips -> section_composition
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Composition Tips ────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_composition
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A few composition principles that work in any visual novel:
narrator: {b}Rule of thirds.{/b} Place characters at 1/3 or 2/3 of the screen, not dead center — unless the moment calls for it.
narrator: {b}Empty space is meaning.{/b} A character alone on a wide shot reads as isolated. Use it deliberately.
narrator: {b}Match framing to mood.{/b} Close for intimate, wide for epic, off-center for unease.
narrator: {b}Background matters.{/b} A cluttered background behind a serious conversation is distracting. Choose backgrounds that support the tone.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And the simplest rule of all:
narrator: {b}If the player's eye goes to the wrong place, the staging is wrong.{/b}
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: You don't need film school. You need to play your scene, notice where your eyes go, and adjust until they go where you want.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Camera and staging cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}Positions:{/b} far_left, left, center, right, far_right, or custom x,y.
narrator: {b}[show char pos expr]{/b} — place a character at a position with an expression.
narrator: {b}[move char pos easing ms]{/b} — animate a character to a new position.
narrator: {b}[camera pan x1,y1 x2,y2 easing ms]{/b} — pan the camera.
narrator: {b}[camera zoom from to easing ms]{/b} — zoom in or out.
narrator: {b}Zoom 1.0{/b} = neutral. 1.2 = close. 0.9 = wide.
narrator: {b}Tip:{/b} Stillness is powerful. Only move the camera when it adds meaning.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Staging is invisible when done right. The player doesn't notice the camera — they just feel the scene.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's stage a few moods. Pick a scenario:
> A tense confrontation -> practice_tense
> A quiet confession -> practice_quiet
> A grand reveal -> practice_reveal

@label practice_tense
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: Imagine two characters facing off. They start far apart...
[move lavender left easeInOut 500]
[wait 500]
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Then slowly close the distance. The camera stays still — the shrinking gap does the work.
Lavender: No zoom needed here. The movement tells the story of escalation.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_quiet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A confession. Close framing. Just you and me.
narrator: The room is quiet. The background fades to unimportance.
[show lavender center emphasis]
Lavender: This is where a gentle zoom from 1.0 to 1.15 over 800ms would say more than any dialogue line.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Pair it with a slower text speed and a longer autodelay. Let the moment breathe.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_reveal
[show lavender far_right talking]
Lavender: The camera pans slowly to the left. Nothing but empty background...
[move lavender center easeOut 600]
[wait 600]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Then I step into frame. The reveal isn't what I say — it's where I appear and when.
Lavender: A slow pan followed by a character show is one of the most effective staging patterns in VNs.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Confrontations are about distance — physical and emotional. Let the blocking carry the tension.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Quiet moments need quiet staging. Small zoom, slow pace, minimal movement.
[else]
Lavender: Reveals use timing and space. The player sees the empty frame first, then the surprise.
[endif]
Lavender: Try staging these in your own script. Position characters, add a camera move, and play it back.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_3]
